Its reduced subscheme is the diagonal $W$. Put

$ cal(L)_p = cal(O)_(Delta_F) \( B_(p \, 1) + B_(p \, 2) + p q S_1 \) . $

Write $h = F compose upright("pr")_1 = F compose upright("pr")_2 : Delta_F arrow.r W'$. On this fiber product,

$ cal(O) \( p S_1 \) tilde.eq h^(*) cal(O)_(W') \( S' \) tilde.eq cal(O) \( p S_2 \) . $

Consequently every pair of weights $a + b = q$ gives the same line bundle:

$ cal(O)_(Delta_F) \( B_(p \, 1) + B_(p \, 2) + p a S_1 + p b S_2 \) tilde.eq cal(L)_p . $

On the diagonal of $W' times W'$, every source twist of Equation (45) becomes $- q S'$. Finite base change and projection formula identify the twisted target as

$ \( cal(E) ⊗ cal(E) \) \( q S' \) tilde.eq h_(*) cal(L)_p . $

The product sections defining the columns restrict to global sections of this one bundle $cal(L)_p$. Their values at a diagonal point therefore lie in the image of $H^0 \( Delta_F \, cal(L)_p \)$ in the corresponding fiber of $h_(*) cal(L)_p$. This proves that the rank at every diagonal point is at most

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(47)")
 $ h^0 \( Delta_F \, cal(L)_p \) . $ <common:diagonal-rank-upper>]

For a rank-$n$ bundle $V$ in characteristic $p$, denote by $A_j \( V \)$ the degree-$j$ part of its symmetric algebra modulo the $p$-th powers of local generators. This construction is independent of frame: the $p$-th power of a linear combination is the sum of the $p$-th powers in characteristic $p$. Each $A_j \( V \)$ is locally free and a quotient of $V^(⊗ j)$. Set

$ u = n \( p - 1 \) \, #h(2em) e_j = "rk" A_j \( V \) \, #h(2em) sum_(j = 0)^u e_j = p^n . $

Filtering $cal(L)_p$ by powers of the ideal of the reduced diagonal in $Delta_F$ gives the graded bundles

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(48)")
 $ cal(G)_j = cal(O)_W \( 2 B_p + p q S \) ⊗ A_j \( Omega_W^1 \) \, #h(2em) 0 lt.eq j lt.eq u . $ <common:diagonal-graded>]

Indeed, in smooth local coordinates the algebra is generated by parameter differences with their $p$-th powers zero, and its degree-one conormal is $Omega_W^1$. This coordinate calculation can be made étale locally or in completions and identifies the global associated graded. This is the diagonal-ideal form of the canonical Frobenius filtration #cite(label("KitadaiSumihiro2008")) Corollary 3.5. The same calculation, using only the bundle from the second factor, filters $F^(*) cal(E)$ with pieces #cite(label("Sun2008")) Theorem 3.7

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(49)")
 $ cal(O)_W \( B_p \) ⊗ A_j \( Omega_W^1 \) . $ <common:Frob-pullback-graded>]

Complementary multiplication is a perfect pairing, giving

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(50)")
 $ A_(u - j) \( V \) tilde.eq A_j \( V \)^or ⊗ \( det V \)^(p - 1) . $ <common:socle-pairing>]

On monomials, each exponent vector pairs with its complement to $\( p - 1 \, dots.h \, p - 1 \)$. The top line transforms by the character $\( det V \)^(p - 1)$: this is immediate on diagonal matrices and hence identifies the character of $upright("GL")_n$ on that line.

==== 9.1.4 One slope bound and one flag polynomial <one-slope-bound-and-one-flag-polynomial>

We bound the sections of every graded bundle in Equation (48) by its rank times the same scalar polynomial in $p$. This uniformity lets us sum the ranks of the graded pieces without an additional factor for their number. We begin with a slope bound on the fixed curve; here slope means ordinary degree divided by rank.

#thm([Lemma 9.4.])[
  #emph[There is a constant $c gt.eq 0$, independent of the sufficiently large residual characteristic $p$ and of $j$, such that for $V = Omega_W^1 \|_C$ one has 
$ mu_max \( A_j \( V \) \) lt.eq \( p - 1 \) K_W dot.op C + n c . $

]
] <common:truncated-slope>

#proof[
  Write $F_C$ for absolute Frobenius of $C$. Langer’s instability estimate #cite(label("Langer2004")) Corollary 2.5 gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(51)")
   $ L_min \( V \) := lim_(e arrow.r oo) p^(- e) mu_min \( F_C^(e *) V \) gt.eq - frac(c, p - 1) . $ <common:Langer-min>]
  To check the uniform constant, the discrepancy from $mu_min \( V \) gt.eq 0$ is at most $\( "rk" V - 1 \) deg A_C \/ \( p - 1 \)$, where $A_C$ is a nef line bundle such that $T_C ⊗ A_C$ is globally generated. Its degree can be bounded in terms of the fixed genus, using a sufficiently positive line bundle. Here the rank is $n$, irrespective of the fact that the base is a curve.
  We also need, for every integer $i gt.eq 0$,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(52)")
   $ mu_min \( V^(⊗ i) \) gt.eq i L_min \( V \) . $ <common:tensor-min>]
  Fix $p$ and $i$. Choose a line bundle $A_e$ of degree $- mu_min \( F_C^(e *) V \) + O \( 1 \)$, rounded up with a fixed genus-dependent additive constant, so that $F_C^(e *) V ⊗ A_e$ is globally generated. This follows from Serre duality: after subtracting any point, its minimum slope can be made greater than $2 g \( C \) - 2$, which annihilates $H^1$ and makes evaluation at that point surjective. For every vector-bundle quotient $Q$ of $V^(⊗ i)$, the bundle $F_C^(e *) Q ⊗ A_e^(⊗ i)$ is then globally generated. Consequently
  $ p^e mu \( Q \) + i deg A_e gt.eq 0 . $
  Divide by $p^e$ and let $e arrow.r oo$ to prove Equation (52). This limit is taken for each fixed $p \, i$; there is no interchange of Frobenius-iteration and characteristic limits.
  Since $A_(u - j) \( V \)$ is a quotient of $V^(⊗ \( u - j \))$, the perfect pairing Equation (50) yields
  $ mu_max \( A_j \( V \) \) & = \( p - 1 \) deg det V - mu_min \( A_(u - j) \( V \) \)\
 & lt.eq \( p - 1 \) K_W dot.op C + frac(\( u - j \) c, p - 1)\
 & lt.eq \( p - 1 \) K_W dot.op C + n c . $
  ◻
]

The divisors $B_p - p L = T_0 - \( p - k_0 N_p \) L$ range over a fixed finite list. Combining Equation (42), Equation (44), Lemma 38 therefore gives, uniformly for $0 lt.eq j lt.eq u$,

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(53)")
 $ mu_max \( cal(G)_j \|_C \) & lt.eq p \( 4 + 2 \/ r \) Lambda H^n + O \( 1 \)\
 & lt.eq M_p := 7 p Lambda H^n + c_0 \, $ <common:common-slope-bound>]

where $c_0 gt.eq 0$ is fixed. The coefficient $7$ provides harmless room in this common bound.

#thm([Lemma 9.5.])[
  #emph[For all sufficiently large $p$, 
$ h^0 \( Delta_F \, cal(L)_p \) lt.eq R #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] 7^n H^n + O \( 1 \/ p \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] < R \/ 4 . $

 The error constant is independent of the filtration index $j$.]
] <common:diagonal-sections>

#proof[
  Write the fixed flag as
  $ W = W_n supset W_(n - 1) supset dots.h.c supset W_1 = C \, #h(2em) W_(n - k) in \| h_k \|_(W_(n - k + 1)) quad \( 1 lt.eq k lt.eq n - 1 \) . $
  At step $k$, fix the preceding nonnegative integers $b_1 \, dots.h \, b_(k - 1)$ and put
  $ cal(F)_k = cal(G)_j \|_(W_(n - k + 1)) #scale(x: 180%, y: 180%)[\(] - sum_(i < k) b_i h_i #scale(x: 180%, y: 180%)[\)] . $
  For each $b_k gt.eq 0$, the divisor restriction sequence is
  $ 0 arrow.r cal(F)_k \( - \( b_k + 1 \) h_k \) arrow.r cal(F)_k \( - b_k h_k \) arrow.r cal(F)_k \( - b_k h_k \) \|_(W_(n - k)) arrow.r 0 . $
  Iterating it for $b_k = 0 \, 1 \, dots.h$ and then proceeding to the next member of the flag bounds the section space by
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(54)")
   $ h^0 \( W \, cal(G)_j \) lt.eq sum_(b_1 \, dots.h \, b_(n - 1) gt.eq 0) h^0 (C \, cal(G)_j \|_C #scale(x: 180%, y: 180%)[\(] - sum_i b_i h_i \|_C #scale(x: 180%, y: 180%)[\)]) . $ <common:flag-lattice-sum>]
  At each stage the remainder is zero after sufficiently negative ample twisting. Its stopping index need not be uniform in $j \, p$ or in the preceding twists: enlarging the nonnegative finite sums to the displayed lattice sum preserves the inequality.
